d explanations are a way to  transfer that knowledge but how much of  that knowledge should be transferred how  to figure out if what part of that  knowledge is furious what part of that  is valuable I think there are some like  gray areas there which need to be  thought through more which unfortunately  I'm not sure if it's happening enough  sure okay  all right okay so I'm going to keep  going so now that we have talked so much  about when and why model understanding  might be useful I'm just going to show a  few applications just to concretize all  of this right and we'll use some nice  simple fun pictures to see the different  use cases in which explanations might be  helpful or model interpretations might  be helpful you'll not only see the  application but you'll also see examples  of what an explanation or interpretation  could look like right so let's say that  now we have a predictive model  uh so which takes his input some images  and then it makes predictions about the  animal that's in the images right and  this model was built by a developer so  you pass this image and then it's  rightly predicting that this image is  Siberian Husky but if you sort of probe  the model a little bit more and see what  are the areas of the image that the  model is focusing on what you see is  that the model is focusing on the snow  pieces right so in some sense while the  prediction looks okay once you see the  features that the model is looking at or  paying attention to you see that it's  actually not looking at the animal but  rather at the background or trying to  find snow in the image right and that's  what it is using to predict the image as  Siberian Husky so in some sense this  understanding is basically what tells  the developers that you know model is  relying on spurious or incorrect  features and you know maybe we need to  debug this model better improve it and  so on right so here is a clear use case  where model understanding is helping  with debugging of models which I am sure  several offers with the researchers  engineer scientists who use machine  learning models can find this incredibly  valuable okay so now let's look at  another use case so where uh there's a  predictive model that is being used by a  particular judicial facility to make  decisions about if people should be  released on bail or not right again I  just want to put a disclaimer out there  that I'm not advocating for any such  applications but unfortunately there  already exists some of these out there  so you know the the way we are thinking  about it is we are basically trying to  analyze that setting a bit more okay all  right so these kinds of models they take  the details of the defendant like for  example the defendants  socioeconomic status the demographic  attributes their past criminal history  and so on and predict if the person  should be released or not or is the  person to too risky to be released or  not right so in this case the model is  taking the details of this defendant and  making a prediction but then this does  not give any information to the judge  about whether or when and how to use  that prediction whether they can rely on  this prediction or not okay but if we  show the judge some of the key features  that the model is focusing on for  example maybe the model is looking at  race and gender when making this  prediction the judge will realize that  this prediction is basically biased it's  being based on features like race and  gender which should not happen so the  judge is going to hopefully discount  such a prediction and make his own  decision right so in this case model  understanding has helped facilitate bias  detection  similarly in another scenario now we can  think about end users so Banks often  have such models which they imply in  order to sort of predict who should be  given a loan right so loan applicants  details or their application will be  passed on to the bank Bank the bank then  runs the predictive model and determines  if a person should get a loan or not now  let's say an applicant is denied a loan  if we just tell that person your loan is  denied sorry that's it that's not very  helpful because now the person doesn't  know what to do anymore right so they  don't have a means for further action  but instead if we actually tell them  increase your salary by say 5 or 10K or  50k pay your credit card bills on time  uh for say about three months then you  know come back and reapply and you'll  get a loan that is actionable  information that the person can use in  order to sort of work on their profile  come back and reapply right so in fact  these kinds of mandations uh have been  encoded in some of the legislations like  gdpr especially for the finance sector  or Banks which are using predictive  models in deciding on loan applications  and other such things uh so in this case  model understanding is helping provide  recourse to individuals who are  adversely affected by model predictions  right and similarly if we think of a  healthcare scenario uh so if there's a  doctor that is relying on model  predictions and the model is basically  taking a bunch of data and labeling  patients as like healthy or sick now the  doctor again if they just look at the  predictions they have no way of knowing  which ones to rely on which ones they  should apply their own judgment to and  so on but if we had like a full  understanding of what the underlying  model is potentially doing so for  example in this case if that's what the  model is exactly doing and the doctor  sees that the doctor can quickly realize  that you know the model is using  irrelevant features like ID numbers when  making predictions on females of  population right and therefore they  should not trust the model's predictions  at least for that subgroup  so in this case model understanding is  helping assess if and when to trust  model predictions when making decisions  right so such an understanding can in  fact also be helpful for regulatory  authorities in deciding if a model  should be sort of approved for broader  deployment or not right so in this case  if an authority like FDA looks at that  explanation of what the model is doing  the authority might realize that okay  this model is just using irrelevant  features on half the population which is  basically all the females right and so  it's not really for deployment at this  point so in this sense model  understanding can also allow us to wet  models to determine if they're suitable  for real world deployment okay so just  to summarize there is obviously a lot of  utility to sort of understanding models  and their predictions for example you  know applications like debugging bias  detection recourse you know if and when  to trust model predictions wetting  models to assist for suitability for  deployment all of these are like use  cases or applications that are enabled  by model understanding and there are  several stakeholders that benefit from  this right so including end users like  ourselves who might apply for loans at  Banks or decision makers like judges  doctors policy makers or regulatory  agencies or researchers scientists  engineers all of us can benefit from  this in some way or the other  okay  right  any quick thoughts questions or we can  move on  great okay so now that we have discussed  so much about where model understanding  can be beneficial when it may not be  beneficial and so on let's take a step  back and think about how can we actually  achieve model understanding right so  while there are you know several  approaches to do this at a very high  level very broadly you can think of two  classes of strategies that are used to  achieve model understanding so one is  build models that are inherently  interpretable right so for example  models like you know linear regressions  logistic regressions or shallow decision  trees or like small rule-based models so  these are all models that are often  