rmation for example  and then  also the example of if you have a really  complex task and you have like a tree  algorithm if it has like a thousand  decisions that are made I wouldn't call  that very accessible so probably the  second one for more complex  yeah  so in some sense uh what you just said  can be summarized using graphs like this  right so what we need to think about is  this kind of a scenario of are there  trade-offs between interpretability and  accuracy right so for example if you are  working with some data or in a setting  where you can maybe build a five level  three and that has a very high accuracy  that's probably good enough and you know  the model is inherently interpretable  and it's also giving you accuracy and  you're done right but in a lot of real  world applications as you rightly  pointed out the data is becoming more  and more complex more and more High  dimensional and large and at which point  these kinds of trade-offs start kicking  in more and more right so in fact for  data sets in NLP vision and even some  very large tabular data sets biomedical  data sets these kinds of data what you  see is that the models that are are  often considered as inherently  interpretable like regressions or trees  they're much less accurate than models  like random Forest which is an ensemble  model or deep neural network with  several intermediate layers and so on  right so as we are running into these  situations where accuracy  interpretability trade-offs exists we  are being kind of forced to choose  post-hoc explanations otherwise we can  just rely on inherently interpretable  models right so what are these kinds of  situations so we just talked a little  bit about you know if your data is too  complex you might have to build a  complex model for the sake of accuracy  then use post-talk explanations right so  let's dig a little bit deeper or at  least give an example of when you might  be able to use inherently interpretable  models and when yo